 // TODO: reference vp8.DECODER_WORKBUF_LEN_MAX_INCL_WORST_CASE, although the
 // worst case for VP8L is bigger than for VP8.
 pub const DECODER_WORKBUF_LEN_MAX_INCL_WORST_CASE : base.u64 = 0x4C00_0000
 
+// The default value of the largest supported VP8-inside-VP8X chunk length.
+// This limitation is a consequence of combining Wuffs' "decode an image" API
+// with WebP/VP8X/VP8's file format design.
+//
+// Wuffs' API only assumes sequential I/O access, not random I/O access, to the
+// encoded image's bytes. It also is incapable (in the security capability
+// sense) of dynamically allocating memory, so any O(width × height) pixel
+// buffers, or any other buffers that aren't O(1) in terms of width and height,
+// must be allocated by the caller, not the callee (Wuffs code).
+//
+// "How much dynamic memory does the caller need to allocate?" is answered by
+// the decoder.workbuf_len method, which is valid to call as soon as
+// decoder.decode_image_config returns (and decode_image_config, unlike
+// decode_frame, does not take a variable-length slice-of-bytes workbuf
+// argument). workbuf_len must return a worst case estimate, as it has only one
+// shot at answering "how much dynamic memory?"
+//
+// On the other hand, an VP8 image file can have multiple "partitions", which
+// means that a VP8 decoder must support random I/O access. Wuffs supports this
+// by, in the worst case, copying the VP8's chunk payload (the encoded image
+// data) into the workbuf. (Copying into a contiguous buffer is the same as
+// what the libwebp C library's WebPIAppend "incremental API" does.) But doing
+// so requires knowing how large the VP8 chunk is, so that the caller can
+// allocate a sufficiently large workbuf.
+//
+// We cannot answer that question precisely until we parse the VP8 chunk, but
+// in the WebP/VP8X file format, the VP8 chunk must occur after any ALPH chunk.
+// For Wuffs to parse an ALPH chunk, it must have already "allocated" enough
+// memory, more than O(1), by answering "how much dynamic memory?"
+//
+// As we cannot answer that question precisely (whilst still satisfying Wuffs'
+// "decode an image" API, which works across numerous image file formats, not
+// just WebP), we use a made-up upper bound, which is this number.
+//
+// Its value is arbitrary, but there is a trade-off. The larger it is, the more
+// workbuf memory Wuffs will say it needs (and callers will allocate), even if
+// it later discovers that it over-budgeted. The smaller it is, Wuffs will
+// reject (as unsupported) more large-but-valid WebP/VP8X/VP8 files.
+//
+// This default value is 8 MiB.
+pub const VP8X_MAX_INCL_VP8_CHUNK_LENGTH_DEFAULT : base.u64 = 8_388608
+
+// TODO: in the future, regarding VP8X_MAX_INCL_VP8_CHUNK_LENGTH_DEFAULT, we
+// could make this set by a quirk instead of hard-coded.
+//
+// Or, we could change the semantics of the wuffs_base__image_decoder API
+// (which applies to multiple image formats, not just WebP). The API is that
+// you can call workbuf_len as soon as decode_image_config returns. workbuf_len
+// returns a range and any value within that range should be a valid length for
+// the "dynamically allocated, not O(1) in terms of width and height" workbuf
+// memory passed to decode_frame.
+//
+// We could relax this so that decode_frame could return a "$short workbuf"
+// suspension, if the workbuf length is unknown at the time of the first
+// decode_frame call, and the caller is expected to essentially realloc the
+// workbuf, keeping any existing contents when growing it.
